digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56945-0
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This study investigates whether published polygenic risk scores yield consistent individual high-risk classification for coronary artery disease, type 2 diabetes, and major depressive disorder in the diverse All of Us cohort, and evaluates whether the PRSmix method, an agnostic integration of several scores, can improve predictive accuracy and stabilize risk assignments.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Evidence on evolving PRS methods, clinical translation, classification variability, benchmarking gaps, and PRSmix integration jointly motivates an individual-level evaluation of PRS consistency and prediction."];
  "N_Clin_1" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: PRS delivered to patients (S6) and companies provide scores (S7)."];
  "N_Clin_2" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Companies provide scores (N_Clin_1) and eMERGE develops reports (S8)."];
  "N_Sol_1" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: AOU cohort used (S23) and PRSmix tested (S24)."];
  "N_Tech_1" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: PRS performance improves (S1) and methods evolve (S2)."];
  "N_Tech_2" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Methods evolve (N_Tech_1) and GWAS size expands (S3)."];
  "N_Tech_3" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: GWAS expands (N_Tech_2) and multi-ancestry helps (S4)."];
  "N_Tech_Final" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Multi-ancestry helps (N_Tech_3) and unique info exists (S5), so PRS iterations differ."];
  "N_Var_1" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Inherent variability exists (S11) and stems from methodology (S12)."];
  "N_Var_2" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Methodology differs (N_Var_1) and extreme value theory applies (S13)."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Methodological differences between risk models provide a plausible explanation for observed reclassification of high-risk patients when new models are introduced."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Observed model-driven reclassification and the difference between AHA PREVENT and PCE equations support the narrower concern that updated risk equations can change risk classification contexts."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) Differences between PREVENT and PCE equations are associated with reclassification of US adults into lower risk categories that can affect treatment eligibility."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) Examples of risk-model reclassification and the existence of different PRS scores for the same traits support concern that score choice may affect high-risk classification stability."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) Differing PRS in emerging clinical-use contexts, together with concern that score choice may affect high-risk classification stability, motivates concern about inconsistent individual risk assignment."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) Untested variability in individual-level high genetic risk classification supports the need for a framework to update or use scores without causing confusion."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) The relevant gap concerns individual consistency because population-level performance metrics may not capture untested variability in individual high-risk classification."];
  "R19" [label="(0,0,0) Individual-level benchmarking of PRS consistency is limited by the historical focus on population-level metrics and by limited availability of diverse datasets."];
  "R20" [label="(0,0,0) Given limitations in individual-level benchmarking and the need to aggregate orthogonal data to maximize predictive performance, a study should evaluate both PRS consistency and predictive performance."];
  "R22" [label="(0,0,0) In the All of Us cohort, testing PRSmix to integrate information across several PRS provides evidence that this integration can mitigate disagreement among published scores in that setting."];
  "R23" [label="(0,0,0) An individual PRS consistency and predictive-performance gap can be addressed by evaluating PRSmix integration of several PRS in the All of Us cohort."];
  "R24" [label="(0,0,0) The repaired problem and strategy motivate investigating whether PRSmix-based integration of several PRS can stabilize individual high-risk classification while preserving or improving prediction in the All of Us cohort."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) PRS reporting efforts are accompanied in some settings by development of clinically valid assays and workflows, rather than proving that all PRS reporting programs share the same workflow."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) PRS clinical translation is being explored through patient reports, workflow development, and clinical trials incorporating PRS into medical decision-making."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Because PRS iterations can differ and PRS clinical translation is being explored, the choice among different PRS is relevant to emerging clinical-use contexts."];
  "S1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently there is improvement in PRS predictive performances over the past decade."];
  "S10" [label="(10,0,0) Currently there are new clinical trials incorporating PRS into medical decision-making."];
  "S11" [label="(11,0,0) Currently there is inherent variability in high-risk classification for complex disease models."];
  "S12" [label="(12,0,0) Currently there are differences in training population and methodology stemming variability."];
  "S13" [label="(13,0,0) Currently there is extreme value theory suggesting models differ in classifying risk extremes."];
  "S14" [label="(14,0,0) Currently there is significant reclassification of high-risk patients with new models."];
  "S15" [label="(15,0,0) Currently there are new AHA PREVENT equations differing from PCE equations."];
  "S16" [label="(16,0,0) Currently there is reclassification of US adults to lower risk categories affecting treatment."];
  "S17" [label="(17,0,0) Currently there are different scores used by studies and companies for the same traits."];
  "S18" [label="(18,0,0) Currently there is a need for a framework to update scores without causing confusion."];
  "S19" [label="(19,0,0) Currently there is untested variability of individual-level classification of high genetic risk."];
  "S2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently there are Bayesian approaches improving variant weights and PRS performance."];
  "S20" [label="(20,0,0) Currently there is a focus on population-level metrics rather than individual consistency."];
  "S21" [label="(21,0,0) Currently there is limited availability of diverse datasets precluding individual-level benchmarking."];
  "S22" [label="(22,0,0) Currently there is a need to aggregate orthogonal data to maximize predictive performance."];
  "S23" [label="(23,0,0) Currently there is use of the All of Us cohort to compare high genetic risk classification."];
  "S24" [label="(24,0,0) Currently there is testing of PRSmix to integrate information across several PRS."];
  "S25" [label="(25,0,0) Currently there is extreme disagreement found between published scores mitigated by PRSmix."];
  "S3" [label="(3,0,0) Currently there is expansion in GWAS size reaching millions of participants."];
